    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A composite deck is often sold as a "set it and forget it" project. It is true that composite boards need far less maintenance than traditional treated timber, but they are not invincible. Installation errors can lead to structural problems, a deck that looks wrong, and claims that the warranty may not cover.</p>
<p>To make sure your new deck does not become an expensive headache, avoid these common mistakes, each of which can lead to costly repairs or even a full rebuild.</p>

<h2 id="spacing">1. Getting the spacing wrong</h2>
<p>One of the most frequent and expensive mistakes is failing to allow for expansion and contraction. Unlike wood, which moves mainly with moisture, composite boards also expand and contract along their length as the temperature changes.</p>
<p>If you butt boards tightly against each other or against the house, they have nowhere to go when the sun heats them. The result can be buckling, warping or boards pulling against their fixings. Always follow the manufacturer's gapping requirements for both end-to-end and side-to-side spacing; side gaps are commonly around 3–6 mm (1/8–1/4 in), but the exact figure depends on the product and the temperature when you install. Get it wrong and a wavy deck surface can be very hard to fix without lifting the boards.</p>

<h2 id="fasteners">2. Using the wrong fixings</h2>
<p>Using the wrong screws is a recipe for trouble. Composite is denser than wood, and standard wood screws can cause "mushrooming", where the material bulges up around the screw head and leaves a rough, unsightly bump.</p>
<p>It is also tempting to save money on cheaper fixings that are not rated for use with pressure-treated timber. The preservatives in treated wood can corrode unsuitable metal over time, leaving boards loose. Use the hidden fastening system or composite-specific screws your board manufacturer recommends, rated for treated timber and outdoor use.</p>

<h2 id="joists">3. Leaving the joists unprotected</h2>
<p>Composite boards typically outlast a timber frame, unless you protect the frame. Many homeowners spend heavily on premium boards but skip joist tape, a waterproof membrane applied to the top edges of the timber joists.</p>
<p>Without it, water can sit where the boards meet the joists and encourage rot. If the frame rots before the boards wear out, you have to lift your perfectly good composite boards to repair the structure, and some may be damaged in the process. Protecting your joists is one of the cheapest forms of insurance you can buy for the deck's lifespan.</p>

<h2 id="substructure">4. Assuming the old frame is good enough</h2>
<p>Composite boards are typically heavier and more flexible than timber boards of the same size. A common mistake is resurfacing an old wooden deck with composite without checking whether the existing frame can take it.</p>
<p>Older timber decks often have joists at 600 mm (24 in) centres. Most composite boards need closer support, typically 400 mm (16 in) or even 300 mm (12 in) centres, and closer still for diagonal or herringbone layouts; check the manufacturer's installation guide. On wider spacing, composite boards can sag and feel bouncy. Before you buy materials, have the frame checked: the footings, posts and joists must be sound, and for a raised deck ask a qualified builder or structural engineer.</p>
<!--figure:landscape-plan-samples|A landscape plan with material samples and notes on an outdoor table: check the frame and the board specification at the planning stage, before you order materials-->

<h2 id="ventilation">5. Blocking ventilation</h2>
<p>Composite decks need to breathe. A common mistake is enclosing the deck right down to the ground with solid fascia boards or stone. Without airflow, moisture builds up underneath, encouraging mould and mildew and speeding up rot in the timber frame.</p>
<p>Leave the clearance and ventilation gaps your decking manufacturer specifies, or use slatted or screened skirting that lets air through. Drainage matters too: the ground under the deck should fall slightly away from the house and be covered with a weed membrane and around 5 cm (2 in) of gravel, so standing water does not create a damp microclimate under your new boards.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Download your board manufacturer's installation guide before you start, and keep it with your receipts. Gapping, joist spacing, fixings and ventilation rules are all in there, and following them is usually what keeps the warranty valid.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I lay composite decking directly over an old wood deck?</h3>
<p>It is not recommended. Laying new boards over old ones traps moisture and can lead to rapid rot. Remove the old boards, inspect and reinforce the frame, then install the composite to the manufacturer's spacing.</p>
<h3>Does composite decking get hotter than wood?</h3>
<p>In strong sun it can feel hotter than many woods, especially in darker colours. Lighter colours, good ventilation and some shade all help.</p>
<h3>Do I really need hidden fasteners?</h3>
<p>Not always. Many boards can be face-screwed with the correct composite screws. Hidden fasteners give a cleaner look, avoid mushrooming and are designed to let the boards move naturally, which is why many manufacturers recommend them.</p>
<h3>How do I clean a composite deck without damaging it?</h3>
<p>Use a soft-bristle brush, warm water and a little mild washing-up liquid. If you use a pressure washer, follow the manufacturer's guidance, using a low setting and a fan nozzle. Avoid harsh chemicals and bleach unless the manufacturer specifies them. Our <a href="/blog/composite-deck-maintenance-by-season">seasonal composite deck maintenance guide</a> has a full routine.</p>
<h3>Will DIY installation affect my warranty?</h3>
<p>It depends on the manufacturer, so read the warranty terms before you start. Many accept DIY installation as long as you follow the installation guide closely. Keep receipts and take photos of the joist spacing, joist tape and gaps in case you ever need to make a claim.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most composite deck problems come from the frame and the installation, not the boards, so getting gaps, fixings, joist protection and ventilation right protects your investment. Next, see <a href="/blog/composite-deck-cost">what drives composite deck cost</a> and how to keep your deck looking good with <a href="/blog/composite-deck-maintenance-by-season">composite deck maintenance by season</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
